            <ArticleTitle>Time perspective and smoking, obesity, and exercise in a community sample.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>171-80</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.5993/AJHB.37.2.4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To examine associations between time perspective and smoking, body mass index, and exercise.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">In this community-based survey, adults reported smoking and exercise habits and weight and height and completed the Zimbardo Time Perspective Inventory.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Subjects (N=265) who were more future oriented reported more frequent exercise, but were more likely to smoke. Fatalistic and hedonistic present orientations were not associated with smoking, obesity, or exercise.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Time perspective is not consistently associated with common health behaviors in adults.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
